## Why a separate method

`docs/reports/tokens.md` measures what the tools print, with characters divided by four, and counts the skill and tool definitions once per session. That answers a product question. It is not what a study participant's agent spends. A provider bills the tool definitions and the system prompt on every request, retries and repair calls cost tokens, and a cached prefix is billed differently. The paper must report totals that a reader could check against a provider invoice, and keep estimates apart (AF33).

This document fixes how Study 1, Study 3 and any full-loop pilot count tokens, and how pilots choose the thresholds and sizes that later get preregistered (AF34). It does not set any threshold itself.

:::agent Accounting constraints
- Provider-reported usage is the only source for reported totals. Label every other count as an estimate and name its method (characters / 4, or a named tokenizer and version).
- Never merge cached and uncached input into one number. Report them side by side.
- No number in this document, in `docs/reports/tokens.md` or in an earlier draft is a study threshold or a sample size until a pilot has produced it and the author has frozen it.
:::

## Units

|Unit|Definition|
|-|-|
|Call|One request to a model provider and its response|
|Episode|All calls an agent makes for one task under one condition and one run, from the first prompt to the final answer|
|Session|The calls that share one conversation state, which may hold several episodes when a protocol reuses context|
|Human turn|A human answer or correction that enters the agent's context during an episode (Study 3 and full-loop pilots)|

The episode is the unit of analysis. A session-level number is reported only when the protocol reuses context across tasks, and then the amortization rule below applies.

## What counts

Every call in an episode is logged and classified. Nothing is dropped because it failed.

|Class|Includes|
|-|-|
|`retrieval`|Calls that ask for a read of the document (outline, section, query, search, line ranges)|
|`repeat-read`|Calls that ask only for reads the episode already made, with the same tool and arguments|
|`repair`|Calls after a tool result that was an error or a view over its budget|
|`answer`|The call that produces the final answer, and any reasoning or output tokens it reports|
|`retry`|Attempts that failed on a transport, rate-limit or server error, logged even when the provider bills nothing|
|`human-context`|Calls that carry a human answer, correction or question into the agent's context (Study 3 and full-loop pilots, not produced by the Study 1 runner)|

A call that asks for a new read right after an error is `repair`, not `retrieval`. A malformed final answer is not repaired. The Study 1 protocol scores it as given, so it counts as wrong.

Setup is not a call class. The system prompt and tool definition are part of every call's input, and they are not subtracted. They are measured on their own by a setup probe, one request per model and tool definition with the system prompt, the tool and a one-line message, so readers can see how much of the input is fixed.

## Per-call record

The model runner (`npm run eval -- run`) keeps every request in the episode's transcript with the provider's usage as returned, including the requests of an answer that failed (`run-<n>.failed-<k>.json`, never overwritten by a retry) and every failed attempt. `npm run eval -- tokens <run dir>` turns the transcripts into one JSON line per call in `calls.jsonl`.

```json
{"episode": "model-a:provider-model-1/s03-warehouse-scanner-spec/s03-t07/S3/run1",
 "model": "model-a:provider-model-1", "doc": "s03-warehouse-scanner-spec", "task": "s03-t07",
 "condition": "S3", "run": "1", "call": 3, "class": "retrieval",
 "usage": {"input": 4120, "output": 96, "cacheRead": 3800, "cacheWrite": null, "reasoning": 0},
 "usage_raw": {"...": "the provider's own fields, unchanged"}, "mapping": "chat-completions",
 "toolCalls": 1, "repeatedReads": 0, "status": 200, "error": null, "ms": 1840}
```

`usage_raw` keeps the provider's fields as returned, so a later reader can redo the mapping. `input` always includes cached input. When a provider reports cache reads and writes beside its input count, they are added to it. A usage shape the mapping does not know is counted as `unmapped` and never guessed. No estimate enters these records. Transcripts are stored next to the log, as the Study 1 protocol requires.

## Totals and reporting

For each episode, report uncached input, cache reads, cache writes, output and reasoning tokens separately, and the total, which is input plus output with cached input counted as ordinary input (decided below). Then, per model and condition,

- the median and interquartile range per answered episode, and the mean with a 95% interval from the same paired bootstrap the scorer uses for correctness, with its difference from the baseline,
- the tokens failed episodes spent, reported beside the per-episode numbers and included in the spend,
- the share spent in each class, so retrieval savings are not hidden by repair costs,
- the number of calls and of `repair`, `repeat-read` and `retry` calls,
- the setup input per call and its share of all input, from the setup probe,
- correctness and token cost together, per task, so a cheaper condition that fails more tasks is visible as such.

`npm run eval -- tokens` writes all of these, with `--scored` taking the scorer's outcomes for the correctness columns.

:::decision{status=accepted date=2026-11-07 owner=@roshanmadhushanka} Cached input counts as ordinary input in the total
The headline total is input plus output, with cache reads and writes counted as plain input tokens. Uncached input, cache reads and cache writes are always reported beside it. A price-weighted total would change when prices change and differ between providers. Cost in money is reported apart, from each provider's prices on the run date.
:::

Correctness is a gate (requirements, research questions section). A token saving is reported for a condition only beside its correctness result, and never as a composite score.

### Amortization

When a protocol reuses a session for several tasks, the per-episode share of `setup` tokens depends on how many tasks the session holds. Report both the measured per-episode numbers and the session total. Any amortized figure states the number of tasks it assumes, and the paper gives the break-even task count rather than one amortized number.

### Authoring and maintenance effort

Tokens an agent spends writing or updating `.smd` markup, and minutes a person spends marking a document, are costs of the approach. Study 2 and the full-loop pilot record them separately from reading costs (AF33). The marking log in the annotation guide gives the marker's time per document.

## Historical numbers

The 2.5.0 and 2.6.0 measurements under the Styled Markdown name keep their executable version and their estimate method. They are not re-labelled, re-scaled or mixed into tables with provider-reported counts (C12). New 3.x runs of `docs/reports/tokens.md` stay estimates and say so.

## Pilot-based thresholds and sizing

Pilots exist to produce the numbers a confirmatory study needs. They are not evidence for a hypothesis, and pilot data never enters the confirmatory analysis.

### What a pilot must report

- The cost per episode and per run in provider tokens and money, per model.
- Correctness per condition and whether any condition sits at ceiling or floor.
- Variance components for documents, tasks within documents, runs and models, from a mixed model fitted to the pilot.
- The rate of harness failures, invalid tool calls and scorer disagreements with a human.

### Choosing thresholds

Each confirmatory comparison states one of three claims before the run, and its margin comes from the pilot and the author's judgment of what matters, not from a draft.

|Claim|Holds when|Used for|
|-|-|-|
|Superiority|The 95% interval of the difference excludes zero in the stated direction|A view or cue that should improve an outcome|
|Non-inferiority|The interval's lower bound stays above minus the margin|A cheaper view that should not lose correctness|
|Equivalence|Both bounds stay inside plus and minus the margin (two one-sided tests)|A claim that two conditions do the same|

Study 1's H1 said "within 5 percentage points" and called it equivalence, while its wording read as non-inferiority.

:::decision{status=accepted date=2026-11-07 owner=@roshanmadhushanka} H1 is a non-inferiority claim
H1 claims that outline plus sections loses no more than the margin against reading the whole file. It holds when the lower bound of the 95% interval of the difference stays above minus the margin. The margin of 5 points is a planning value that the author confirms or replaces after the pilot, before preregistration.
:::

The 40% token reduction and the 10-point power target are planning values too, set after the pilot.

### Sizing

Size each confirmatory study by simulating the planned mixed model with the pilot's variance components, at the chosen margin, for 80% power unless the author sets another target. Observations are clustered by document, task and model, so the simulation resamples at those levels and never treats runs as independent. Report the simulation code, seed and result in the preregistration.

### Freeze list

Before any confirmatory run, record these in the preregistration and the corpus freeze record. Any change after the run starts restarts it.

- Corpus version, annotation guide version, task file hash.
- This method's version and the commit of `npm run eval -- tokens`.
- Tool commit and executable version for every condition.
- Prompts, tool definitions and skill text, by hash.
- Model ids with provider version or date, sampling settings and seeds where supported.
- The `today` clock for views and the tokenizer used for any estimate.
- Conditions, ablations, the scorer commit and the judge prompt.
- Claims, margins, analysis model, exclusion rules and planned sample size.

## Open questions

:::question Provider budget and models
The author decides providers, models and spend for the pilot and the confirmatory runs. Until then the pilot plan assumes one model and four documents, as the Study 1 protocol says.
:::

## What is left before the freeze

The method is complete except for the provider decision above. It is frozen, with its version, in the preregistration once the pilot has run and the author has set the margins, the claim of each comparison and the sample size.
